    {
      "id": "8a214967-4928-4398-be40-7835f2997abc",
      "title": "Thesis 3: The Tamil Nadu Food Processing Compliance & Traceability Stack",
      "description": "The problem. Tamil Nadu has a growing food processing sector — spice mills, snack manufacturers, dairy units, coconut processing. These units must comply with FSSAI licensing, GST, local municipal health permits, and increasingly, export traceability requirements. A spice exporter in Erode must prove the origin of every batch. A snack manufacturer in Madurai must track oil quality. This is done on paper. When a buyer asks for traceability, the unit owner cannot produce it.\n\nWhy now. Two forces:\n\nExport demand: Global buyers (EU, US, Middle East) now require digital traceability for food imports. A Tamil Nadu spice exporter who cannot provide batch-level traceability will lose the contract.\n\nFSSAI digitisation: FSSAI is moving toward digital compliance, but the tooling for small units is nonexistent.\n\nWhy existing solutions fail. Traceability software (e.g., IBM Food Trust, SAP) is built for large exporters. A 15-person spice mill cannot afford it. The gap is a ₹2,000/month traceability layer that works on a phone, speaks Tamil, and integrates with FSSAI filing.\n\nYour wedge. Start with one product and one export corridor: black pepper or turmeric from Erode to the EU. Build a QR-code-based batch tracking system. The farmer or aggregator scans a QR code at harvest. The mill scans at intake. The packer scans at packing. The exporter scans at shipping. The EU buyer scans the final QR and sees the full chain. Charge the exporter ₹5,000 per shipment. Charge the mill ₹2,000/month for the software.\n\nStack architecture.\n\nData layer: A batch ID schema that links farmer → aggregator → mill → packer → exporter. Each scan appends to an immutable log.\n\nCompliance layer: A rules engine that maps the batch data to FSSAI and EU import requirements. This is your regulatory compiler applied to food.\n\nAPI layer (the stack): Other developers can build mobile apps for farmers, ERP integrations for mills, or financing products for exporters on top of your batch API.\n\nDistribution layer: Partner with an export house. They have the buyer relationships. You provide the tech. Revenue share.\n\n90-day MVP. Weeks 1–4: Interview 5 spice exporters in Erode. Understand their current traceability process (likely a notebook). Weeks 5–10: Build a minimal QR-code scan app (React Native or Flutter) + a web dashboard showing batch history. Weeks 11–12: Run one shipment through the system with a friendly exporter. Show the EU buyer the QR page.\n\nFirst 10 customers. Export houses, not mills. They are the ones who lose contracts if traceability fails. Ask: \"Can I digitize your traceability for one shipment for free? If the buyer accepts it, we talk about pricing.\" One export house can bring 10 mills onto the platform.\n\nBusiness model. Per-shipment fee (₹5,000) + mill SaaS (₹2,000/month). The per-shipment fee is the wedge. The SaaS is the recurring revenue. At 20 export houses × 10 shipments/month = ₹10 lakh/month in shipment fees alone.\n\nMoat. The batch data network. Once you have 500 shipments traced through your system, you have a dataset of Tamil Nadu spice quality, origin, and logistics. This is valuable to buyers, insurers, and financiers. You become the trust layer for Tamil Nadu food exports.\n\nRisks. (1) Exporters may not pay. Mitigate by starting with one buyer who demands traceability. (2) QR scanning at the farmer level is hard. Mitigate by starting at the mill level, not the farm. (3) Regulatory changes. Your compiler layer handles this.",
      "status": "idea",
      "priority": "medium",
      "tagIds": [
        "65b949e7-06d0-445e-9caf-e7f532c76726"
      ],
      "pinned": false,
      "position": 2,
      "createdAt": "2026-09-24T20:03:14.300Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-09-24T20:03:58.451Z",
      "comments": [],
      "history": [
        {
          "id": "4217e8fe-ab10-47d1-9c74-6ecf09c4d3e3",
